{
  "id": "grc-4",
  "lang": "grc",
  "title": "Present verbs",
  "unattested": ["λύεις", "λύομεν"],
  "steps": [
    {
      "step": "hear",
      "intro": "One Greek word can say “we believe” or “they have”: the ending tells you who. Read each aloud and notice how the ends change.",
      "cards": [
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "👀", "text": "βλέπω.", "say": "BLE-poh", "en": "I see." },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "👂", "text": "ἀκούεις.", "say": "a-KOO-ays", "en": "You hear. (one person)" },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "✍️", "text": "γράφει.", "say": "GRA-fay", "en": "He writes. (or she, or it)" },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "🙏", "text": "πιστεύομεν.", "say": "pis-TEU-o-men", "en": "We believe." },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "🗣️", "text": "λέγετε.", "say": "LE-ge-te", "en": "You say. (more than one person)" },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "🍞", "text": "ἔχουσιν ἄρτον.", "say": "E-khoo-sin AR-ton", "en": "They have bread." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "step": "notice",
      "cards": [
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "The ending tells you who",
          "body": [
            "A Greek verb is a stem plus an ending. The stem gives the meaning (λυ-, loose); the ending says who is doing it. So Greek doesn't need a separate word for I, you or they.",
            "Learn these six endings: they appear on thousands of verbs."
          ],
          "table": {
            "head": ["who", "ending", "λύω, I loose"],
            "rows": [
              ["I", "-ω", "λύω"],
              ["you (one)", "-εις", "λύεις"],
              ["he, she, it", "-ει", "λύει"],
              ["we", "-ομεν", "λύομεν"],
              ["you (more than one)", "-ετε", "λύετε"],
              ["they", "-ουσιν", "λύουσιν"]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "What the present means",
          "body": [
            "The present describes an action as going on: λύω is “I loose” or “I am loosing”. Pick whichever sounds natural in English.",
            "Dictionaries list a verb by its “I” form: look up λύω, not λύει. That's also why the word lists in this app give “I see” forms.",
            "The “they” ending is sometimes -ουσι, without the ν, when the next word starts with a consonant. It's the same ending.",
            "οὐ means not, and stands before the verb: οὐ βλέπω, I do not see. Before a vowel it becomes οὐκ, and before a rough breathing οὐχ."
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "t": "λύω", "say": "LÜ-oh", "en": "I loose, I am loosing" },
            { "t": "οὐ βλέπω", "say": "oo BLE-poh", "en": "I do not see" },
            { "t": "οὐκ ἔχουσιν", "say": "ook E-khoo-sin", "en": "they do not have" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "step": "practise",
      "cards": [
        {
          "type": "choose",
          "prompt": "What does βλέπομεν mean?",
          "options": ["we see", "I see", "they see", "you see"],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "-ομεν is “we”: βλέπομεν, we see."
        },
        {
          "type": "choose",
          "prompt": "Which word means “they hear”?",
          "options": ["ἀκούουσιν", "ἀκούομεν", "ἀκούει", "ἀκούετε"],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "-ουσιν is “they”: ἀκούουσιν, they hear."
        },
        {
          "type": "match",
          "prompt": "Match each ending to who is doing it.",
          "pairs": [["-ω", "I"], ["-εις", "you (one)"], ["-ει", "he, she, it"], ["-ομεν", "we"], ["-ετε", "you (more than one)"], ["-ουσιν", "they"]]
        },
        {
          "type": "parse",
          "prompt": "Describe ἀκούετε.",
          "word": "ἀκούετε",
          "fields": [
            { "label": "Who?", "options": ["I", "you", "he, she or it", "we", "they"], "answer": "you" },
            { "label": "How many?", "options": ["one", "more than one"], "answer": "more than one" }
          ],
          "explain": "-ετε is “you”, more than one person: ἀκούετε, you (all) hear. Grammar books call it second person plural."
        },
        {
          "type": "parse",
          "prompt": "Describe λαμβάνει.",
          "word": "λαμβάνει",
          "fields": [
            { "label": "Who?", "options": ["I", "you", "he, she or it", "we", "they"], "answer": "he, she or it" },
            { "label": "How many?", "options": ["one", "more than one"], "answer": "one" }
          ],
          "explain": "-ει is “he, she or it”: λαμβάνει, he takes (third person singular). The sentence around it shows which."
        },
        {
          "type": "build",
          "prompt": "Make “we believe” from the stem and an ending.",
          "tiles": ["πιστεύ-", "-ομεν"],
          "join": "",
          "explain": "πιστεύ + ομεν = πιστεύομεν, we believe."
        },
        {
          "type": "choose",
          "prompt": "What does οὐ γινώσκετε mean?",
          "options": ["you do not know", "you know", "we do not know", "they know"],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "οὐ is not, and -ετε is “you” (more than one): you do not know."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "step": "read",
      "intro": "Find the verbs: look at the ending to see who is acting.",
      "cards": [
        { "type": "verse", "ref": "JHN 10:27", "note": "ἀκούουσιν (they hear) and γινώσκω (I know): “My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me.”" },
        { "type": "verse", "ref": "MRK 9:24", "note": "In the middle, after the raised dot, one word: πιστεύω, “I believe”. The father goes on: “help my unbelief!”" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "step": "review",
      "cards": [
        {
          "type": "words",
          "items": [
